**Capital Marketplace — Guild Regulations (first sketch)**

These are the standing rules every topside stall operates under. They are enforced by the Marketplace Wardens (under Lady Elara, The Fox) more than by any single craft guild. Most vendors belong to a loose trade association rather than a strict guild; the rules below are the common floor.

### 1. License & Pitch
- Every permanent or semi-permanent stall requires a **Marketplace License**, renewed yearly.
- The license is a stamped paper + a small brass token hung visibly on the stall.
- Temporary day-pitch sellers (blanket traders, food carts) pay a daily fee instead and get a chalk mark on the nearest pillar.
- Unlicensed selling is fined once, then goods are seized.

### 2. Weights, Measures & Honest Label
- All scales and measures must match the Citadel standard (checked twice a year).
- Food and drink must be what the sign claims. “Healing draught” that does nothing is a fine; one that actively harms is expulsion + possible criminal charge.
- Second-hand goods must be marked as such if the defect is not obvious.

### 3. Banned & Restricted Goods (topside only)
- Open soul-economy trades (memories, experiences, crystallized cards) are **illegal above ground**. That traffic belongs in the Underground Bazaar.
- Live dangerous creatures above a certain size require a special permit.
- Anything that visibly warps local reality (minor rift samples, unstable resin, etc.) needs registration with the Marketplace Wardens.
- Weapons may be sold, but open carry of anything larger than a short blade inside the inner Marketplace is discouraged and can get you moved along.

### 4. Fees & Tithes
- Annual license fee (scale by stall size — still open numbers).
- A small percentage of daily take goes to the Marketplace common fund (maintenance, wardens, lamp oil). Most vendors treat it as the cost of doing business.
- Gate entry fees (the paper passes we already set) are separate and collected at the wall.

### 5. Dispute & Enforcement
- First complaint: verbal warning from a Warden.
- Second: fine.
- Third or serious: stall closed for a period or license revoked.
- The Wardens are practical, not zealous. They care more about public order and Elara’s revenue than moral purity.
- Magic in the open Marketplace is tolerated if it is quiet and commercial. Flashy or dangerous working gets shut down fast.

**Marketplace License Fees (annual, per stall)**

| Stall Type              | Annual Fee | Notes                                      |
|-------------------------|------------|--------------------------------------------|
| Day pitch / blanket     | 5 silver/day | Chalk mark only, no permanent token       |
| Small fixed stall       | 8 gold     | One table or narrow frontage               |
| Standard stall          | 15 gold    | Typical covered stall                      |
| Large / corner stall    | 30 gold    | Extra space, high-traffic location         |
| Multi-stall operation   | 50 gold    | Two or more adjacent pitches under one license |

- Fees are paid to the Marketplace Wardens (Lady Elara’s office).  
- Late renewal: +50% after 30 days, then license suspended.  
- The common-fund percentage (daily take) remains separate and is still open — we can set that later if needed.

These sit comfortably below the cost of actual gear while still feeling like a real annual cost for a working vendor.


### 6. What the rules deliberately leave alone
- The Underground Bazaar and everything beneath it.
- Private deals inside privacy tents or back rooms.
- The Pig’s debt system.
- Most of the actual smuggling (Cellar Custodians territory).

